          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which chapter holds high blood pressure, and what does that chapter deliberately exclude?",
          "answer": "**Chapter 9, diseases of the circulatory system (I00–I99).** Essential (primary) hypertension is code I10, in the hypertensive-disease block that runs from I10 to I16. Those codes also cover combinations of hypertension with heart or kidney disease attributed to it. Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*, and *ICD-10-CM Chapter 9*.\n\n**What sits elsewhere:**\n\n- **A raised blood-pressure reading without a diagnosis of hypertension** is classed as a symptom or finding, in Chapter 18 (R00–R99). Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 18*.\n- **Transient ischemic attack (TIA)** is in the nervous system chapter, at G45, even though it is discussed alongside stroke. Stroke itself (cerebral infarction and intracranial haemorrhage) is circulatory. Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 6*.\n- **Obstructive sleep apnoea** is classed with the nervous system, not the respiratory chapter. Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 10*.\n\n**What the sources do not give:** The retrieved material does not contain a list of what Chapter 9 excludes, so I cannot set one out for you. Two related points do appear in the sources. Pulmonary hypertension is a circulatory disease, so it is included in Chapter 9 rather than excluded. The older split between benign and malignant hypertension was not carried into the current US classification. For the full exclusion notes, check the official ICD-10-CM tabular list or ask a medical coder.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
